@startuml CloudWarehouse_Physical_Architecture
title Physical Architecture — Runtime and Nodes

skinparam node {
  BackgroundColor #F0F8FF
  BorderColor #2980b9
  FontSize 11
}
skinparam component {
  BackgroundColor #ECF0F1
  BorderColor #34495e
  FontSize 10
}

node "Client PC" as Client {
  component "Chrome / Edge" as Browser
}

node "Developer workstation\n(single-machine demo)" as Workstation {
  component "Kestrel host\nCloudWarehouse.Backend\n:5001 HTTP" as Kestrel
  component "SQL Server instance\n:1433 TDS" as MSSQL
}

cloud "GitHub Cloud" as GH {
  component "Git repository" as Repo
  component "Actions runner\nubuntu-latest" as Runner
}

Browser -[#2980b9,thickness=2]-> Kestrel : HTTP :5001
Kestrel -[#27ae60,thickness=2]-> MSSQL : ADO.NET / TCP 1433

actor "Developer" as Dev
Dev -[#E74C3C,dashed]-> Repo : git push (HTTPS)
Runner -[#34495e,dashed]-> Repo : git clone
Runner -[#34495e,dashed]-> Runner : dotnet test\n(no link to local DB)

note right of Kestrel
  Static files: wwwroot/
  Config: appsettings.json
  DDL: database/schema.sql
end note

note bottom of Workstation
  Future production option:
  IIS reverse proxy + dedicated DB server
  (out of course MVP scope)
end note

@enduml

' 1. Demo runs on one machine: browser talks to Kestrel, Kestrel talks to SQL Server.
' 2. CI runs on GitHub-hosted Ubuntu; it builds and tests code without touching our local database.
' 3. The same codebase can later move to IIS plus a separate database server.
